3. A1.4 FIELD FIX (the authorized scope). resolve.go:155-211:
   - SaveDuplicateDecision succeeds, THEN the handler re-reads via
     deps.Decisions.LoadDuplicateDecisions() (resolve.go:165) and reports
     stored[key].SuppressedHash (line 175). SaveDuplicateDecision takes the
     decision BY VALUE (duplicate_decisions.go:89), and canonicalKey rekeys
     SuppressedHash to a StableID on the callee's copy
     (duplicate_decisions.go:107-108), so the re-read is the only path to
     the persisted value. Independent byte-level check:
     TestResolveDuplicatesReportsThePersistedSuppressedHash (resolve_test.go:366)
     reads duplicate_decisions.json with encoding/json directly (its own
     fileDecision/fileDoc types, lines 392-399) and asserts
     out.SuppressedHash == got.SuppressedHash (line 418). Count guard at
     line 408 (len(doc.Decisions)==0 fatals) precedes the lookup, satisfying
     ruling 2026-08-16e.
   - Re-read failure path (lines 166-173): sets `note` and falls back to the
     posted decision.SuppressedHash; does NOT return an error. A successful
     save is never turned into a tool error. Absent-key path (lines 176-188)
     behaves the same. Neither silently claims a value it could not verify.
   - Schema description (resolve.go:26) now reads "the stored identity of the
     suppressed side as it is persisted in duplicate_decisions.json;
     SaveDuplicateDecision rekeys the posted hash to the row's StableID, so
     this may be a StableID rather than the legacy content hash
     list_duplicates reports." Verified TRUE against the code: the reported
     value comes from the re-read of the persisted file, and
